## 1. Introduction
Peptide drugs are made up of short chains of amino acids (AA), usually less than 50, which can be rationally designed to affect protein interactions of interest [1,2]. These drugs have aroused great interest in pharmacology due to their higher specificity and affinity for target proteins and their lower production complexity and cost compared with traditional small molecule drugs and whole protein drugs [3]. Derived from physiologically active biomacromolecules, peptide drugs are highly selective and safer than small molecule drugs [4,5]. Compared with recombinant proteins and antibodies, peptide drugs are normally less immunogenic and more able to penetrate into cells and tissues [6]. In recent decades, therapeutic peptides have been used widely in medicine, with over 60 peptide drugs approved by the Food and Drug Administration (FDA) [7]. The peptide drug market has been expected to exceed US $\$70$ billion in 2019, and there are over 140 peptide drugs currently in clinical trials [7,8]. For example, human natural salivary peptides were found to have antitumor activities against T-47D breast cancer [9]. In addition, some peptides can be used to defend against microorganisms, known as antimicrobial peptides, such as defensins [10,11], NRC-03 and NRC-07 [12]. Among these potent peptide drugs, peptide 46 is a p53 activator that can bind the core and the C-terminal domain of the p53 protein and restore the growth suppressor function in cancer cells [13]. However, despite their high potency, currently there are several drawbacks to peptide drugs. If not stored properly, peptide drugs are easy to be hydrolyzed, oxidized and affected by other degradation factors; hence, their limited chemical and physical stabilities have severely hindered the applications of peptide drugs, especially in developing countries where consistent cold-storage conditions cannot be easily accessed [14]. As a result, strict conditions are generally required for the transportation, usage and storage of peptide drugs, such as low temperature, reduced freeze-thaw cycles and a proteinase-free environment. To address the limited stability issue of peptide drugs, various synthetic carrier systems, such as polymer nanoparticles and nanoemulsions, have been developed to protect peptide drugs before administration [15]. Nevertheless, these nanomatrices often have complex production processes, with undesirable conditions for the peptides during preparation or difficulties in removing the matrices thoroughly [16]. It remains a significant challenge to find a facile, mild and reversible method for synthesizing protective matrices for peptide drugs.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are a new class of porous materials with a tunable pore size and functionality, which have been used widely in catalysis [17,18], separation [19], gas storage [20], and biotechnology applications [21–23]. Importantly, our previous work and the work of others have shown that some MOFs can be easily synthesized in the presence of biomacromolecules in a biofriendly conditions [24–27]. In return, MOFs can provide strong protection for biomacromolecules against harsh conditions and can also release them quickly on demand [27]. These merits make MOF a good candidate for protecting biopharmaceuticals without the need for strict cold-storage conditions or a proteinase-free environment. For example, Chen and coworkers recently employed a MOFs-based strategy to protect antibodies in vitro [28]. However, considering the relatively smaller size and limited stability, no research has been performed yet on protecting peptide drugs with MOFs. Our previous report showed that a wide selection of amino acids could rapidly induce the formation of MOFs in aqueous conditions [29], which suggests the possibility that even smaller biomolecules, such as peptide drugs, could possess the ability to be encapsulated in MOFs in situ in mild synthetic conditions. Here, we developed an effective method using MOFs (zeolitic imidazolate framework-8, ZIF-8) to preserve the activities of a peptide drug (peptide 46, a p53 activator) against high temperature $(80^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ , continued freeze-thaw cycles (15 cycles from $-20^{\circ}C$ to $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ ) and the presence of proteinase (Fig. 1). ZIF-8 was used in this study because of its small pore size $(0.34\ \mathrm{nm})$ , ability to biomineralize in the presence of biomolecules under mild conditions, and easy removal on demand [27]. In addition, ZIF-8 possesses hydrophobic pore environments that could effectively exclude polar solvents such as water, which is responsible for the undesired hydrolysis of peptides [30,31]. Peptide 46 $(4.4~\mathrm{kDa})$ , a p53 activator peptide drug, was adopted in this study since it can penetrate the cell membrane easily and inhibit the growth of cancer cells [32,33]. Our results showed that the peptides released from ZIF-8 retained their structures and full biological activities after being exposed to high temperature, repeated freeze-thaw cycles and enzyme degradation. This study provides an alternative, effective method for the storage of biopharmaceuticals and for the enhancement of their stability.
## 2. Materials and methods
## 2.1. Materials
Zinc acetate dihydrate $(\geq98\%$ , 2-methylimidazole (2-HmIm, $99\%$ , WST-1 assay kit, Atto 647 N NHS ester $(\geq85\%)$ and propidium iodide $:94\%$ , staining dead cells) were purchased from SigmaAldrich (Australia). Calcein-AM $(\geq96.0\%$ , for staining live cells) and Hoechst 33,258 $(\geq98.0\%$ , for cell nucleus staining) were purchased from Santa Cruz Biotechnology (Australia). The p53 activator peptide 46 $(\geq95\%)$ was synthesized by ChinaPeptides Co. LTD (China), and the sequence is GSRAHSSHLKSKKGQSTSRHKKWKMR RNQFWVKVQRG. Dulbecco’s modified eagle medium (DMEM) and Dulbecco’s phosphate-buffered saline (DPBS) for cell culture were purchased from Gibco, ThermoFisher (Australia). Reagents for quantitative polymerase chain reaction (qPCR) were purchased from New England Biolabs (Australia). Primers for qPCR were synthesized by Integrated DNA Technologies. The sequences are shown below:
GAPDH forward primer: CACCACCATGGAGAAGGCTGG.   
GAPDH reverse primer: CCAAAGTTGTCATGGATGACC.   
p53 forward primer: CGAGTCCCGCGGTAATTCTT.   
p53 reverse primer: TGCAGAAGAGGTGCAAGACC.
## 2.2. Synthesis of the Atto 647N NHS-ester labeled-peptide 46
One mg of peptide 46 was dissolved in $0.5~\mathrm{mL}$ bicarbonate buffer (0.1 M, $\mathsf{p H}8.3\mathsf{\cdot}$ , after which $5~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ Atto 647N NHS ester dye ( $\mathrm{\Delta}1~\mathrm{\mathrm{mg/mL}}$ in dry DMSO) was added into the peptide solution. The mixture was incubated and kept shaking for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ at room temperature. The active ester of the Atto $647~\mathsf{N}$ fluorescence dye reacts with the amino groups of the peptide under mild conditions. Gel permeation chromatography (SephadexTM G-25) was then used to separate the labeled peptide from the unreacted dye. The labeled peptide was kept in dark.
## 2.3. Synthesis and characterization of peptide-loaded ZIF-8
One mg of peptide was dissolved in $200~\ensuremath{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ of solution $40\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of Acetonitrile $+~160~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of deionized water (DI water)), after which $100~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of the peptide solution was added to ${800}~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of the 2-HmIm $(0.8~\mathrm{M})$ solution, followed by the addition of $100~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ zinc acetate solution $(0.04\mathrm{~M})$ . Next, the mixture was shaken for 30 mins before centrifugation at $6000~\mathrm{rpm}$ for 10 mins. The supernatant was removed, and the sediment was washed three times with DI water. To determine whether the peptides were encapsulated into ZIF-8, a sodium dodecyl sulfate-polyacrylamide gel electrophoresis (SDS-PAGE) experiment was performed. The protein analysis by SDS-PAGE was performed as described elsewhere [34]. A $20\%$ polyacrylamide gel with a thickness of $0.75\ \mathrm{mm}$ (T Spacer, Hoefer, USA) was prepared. Ten $\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of untreated peptide 46 $(0.5\mathrm{\mg/mL})$ and Peptide 46 after acidic release from ZIF-8 $\mathrm{\langle0.5~mg/mL\rangle}$ were diluted with $10~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of $2\mathrm{x}$ Laemmli buffer (Sigma
Aldrich) and heated in a boiling water bath for 10 mins. These $20~\ensuremath{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ mixtures were loaded into separated pockets of the gel, and $5~{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ of pre-stained protein ladder $\mathrm{10-170~kDa}^{\prime}$ ) was used as marker. After running the gel at $90\mathrm{V}$ for 60 mins and then $200\mathrm{V}$ for 80 mins, the gel was stained with Coomassie Brilliant Blue G. The SDS-PAGE was imaged with a Molecular Imager Gel Doc XR (Bio Rad).
The Atto 647 N NHS-ester labeled-peptide 46 was used to calculate the encapsulation efficiency in ZIF-8 according to the following equation:
encapsulation efficienc $J(\%)=(X-Y)/X*100\%$ where X is the fluorescence intensity of the Atto 647 N-labeled peptides in the solution before encapsulation and Y is the fluorescence intensity of the supernatants after the encapsulation.
## 2.4. Peptides exposed to extreme conditions
Free peptides or peptides-ZIF-8 were exposed to the following specific conditions. For the high temperature condition, the solutions of free peptides and peptides-ZIF-8 were incubated at $80~^{\circ}C$ for 15 mins. For the freeze-thaw cycle conditions, the solutions of free peptides and peptides-ZIF-8 were incubated at $-20^{\circ}C$ for $^{3\mathrm{~h~}}$ , then incubated at $37^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ ; the freeze-thaw cycles were repeated 15 times. For the exposure to proteinase condition, the free peptides and peptides-ZIF-8 were exposed to a $2.5\:\mathrm{mg/mL}$ proteinase solution for 30 mins; to remove the proteinase, the solution was filtered by desalting columns (7 K MWCO, Thermo Scientific Zeba Spin Desalting Columns).
## 2.5. Characterization methods
Scanning electron microscope (SEM) images were taken by a FEI Nova NanoSEM 230 FE-SEM at a voltage of $15~\mathrm{kV}$ with a $4.6~\mathrm{mm}$ working distance. The samples were sputter coated with $5\ \mathrm{nm}$ $\mathrm{Cr}+10\mathrm{nm}\mathrm{Pt}$ under vacuum before the imaging. The $\mathsf{X}.$ -ray diffraction (XRD) measurements were recorded with an XRD-Empyrean I with scattering angles of $5^{\circ}-40^{\circ}~2\theta$ . Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) measurements were performed using a Bruker FT-NIR/IR Spectrometer (Bruker IFS66/S) from a wavenumber of 400 to $4000~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ with a $2~{\mathsf{c m}}^{-1}$ spectral resolution. A Nikon A1 intravital system was used for confocal laser scanning microscopy images (CLSM). Fluorescence and bright-field images for live/dead experiments were taken with an Olympus IX53 inverted microscope. A CLARIOstar Plus microplate reader was used to test the fluorescence intensity in the release kinetics experiments.
## 2.6. Peptide release from ZIF-8
After the above treatments, the peptides were released from ZIF-8. To this end, the peptides-ZIF-8 were immersed into a $\mathsf{p H}5$ acetic buffer solution for 30 mins to remove ZIF-8, followed by dialysis against a $\mathsf{p H}7.4$ phosphate buffer solution $(50~\mathrm{mM})$ for $^{8\mathrm{~h~}}$ in an ice-bath to remove the free zinc ions and 2-HmIm residues. A NanoDrop was used to determine the concentration of the obtained peptides at $\mathsf{A205~n m}$ (specific absorbance peak for peptide bonds). To further verify the integrity of the peptides released from ZIF-8, a QStar Elite Time-of-Flight (TOF) mass spectrometer was employed to analyze the composition of the samples. A Jasco J-815 spectropolarimeter was used to analyze the secondary structure of the peptides. In terms of the peptide release kinetics at $\mathsf{p H}5$ and $\mathsf{p H}7.4$ , fluorescence-labeled peptides were used for the release process. The supernatants were collected, and the fluorescence intensity was assessed by a microplate reader. The data was fitted using a Korsmeyer–Peppas model [35]:
$$
R\%=k t^{n}
$$
where $R\%$ is the percentage of fluorescent peptides released at time t, and k and n are the kinetic constant and release exponent, respectively, that characterize the release mechanism.
## 2.7. Determination of the biological activities of the peptides
After the treatments and characterizations, it was determined whether the treated peptides or peptides released from ZIF-8 kept their biological activities of enhancing the expression p53 and inhibiting the growth of cancer cells. MCF-7 cells (purchased from ATCC) were first cultured in fresh DMEM $(10\%\mathrm{FBS}+1\%$ penicillinstreptomycin) in a 24-well plate in a ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ incubator for $24\mathrm{h}$ (initial cell number: $1\times10^{4}$ cells/well). The medium was then changed to the prepared DMEM medium with untreated or treated peptides (treated peptides included free peptides after exposure to the treatments and peptides released from MOF after experiencing the treatments) at $20~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ , followed by incubation for $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ . Next, WST-1 and live/dead experiments were performed to determine the cell viability. Total RNA was extracted from the cells of the different groups to analyze the p53 gene expression using qPCR analysis. All results were presented as the mean $\pm$ standard deviation with at least 3 samples in each group. One-way ANOVA was adopted for comparisons assuming equal variances, followed by the least significant difference (LSD) test. Unless otherwise stated, $\mathsf{p}<0.001$ was considered highly statistically significant.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Peptide encapsulation in ZIF-8
For ZIF-8 formation, the peptide solution was mixed well with the ligand $2\mathrm{-HmIm})$ solution first, after which the zinc solution was added to induce the biomineralization of ZIF-8. Confocal laser scanning microscopy was used to confirm the encapsulation of the Atto 647 N NHS-ester labeled-peptide 46 (Fig. S1). The fluorescence intensity of the supernatants after encapsulation was determined to calculate the encapsulation efficiency of the peptide drugs, which indicated an $87\%$ encapsulation efficiency. Whether the peptides were encapsulated into ZIF-8 was further determined by the FTIR (Fig. S2) and SDS-PAGE electrophoresis (Fig. 2a). The FTIR results showed the coexistence of peptide peaks (Amide I at $1650~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and Amide II at $1530~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ ) and ZIF-8 peaks $\mathsf{C}{=}\mathsf{N}$ at $1600~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and ZIF ring at $1420{-}1460\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}.$ ). Interestingly, the blue-shifted $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ peak on the peptide was found in the peptideZIF-8 composite, which could be explained by the interaction between zinc and the carboxylate group of peptides. From our previous research, we know that these interactions would improve the stability of the ZIFs [26,36]. The SDS-PAGE results indicated that the peptides released from ZIF-8 had a similar size to those before encapsulation into ZIF-8, which demonstrated that the peptides were encapsulated into ZIF-8 and released without the occurrence of cleavage.
## 3.2. Exposure to extreme conditions
The encapsulated and free peptide drugs were then subjected to different treatments, namely: (1) $80~^{\circ}C$ heating for 15 mins (peptides encapsulated in MOF, P-MOF-HT; free peptides, P-HT); (2) 15 freeze–thaw cycles (each cycle: $^{3\mathrm{~h~}}$ at $-20^{\circ}C$ , followed by $^\mathrm{~2~h~}$ at $37^{\circ}\mathsf C$ ) (peptides encapsulated in MOF, P-MOF-FT; free peptides, P-FT); and (3) exposure to a proteinase solution $(2.5~\mathrm{mg/mL})$ for 30 mins (peptides encapsulated in MOF, P-MOFE; free peptides, P-E). After these treatments, the shape and morphology of these ZIF-8 particles were investigated by SEM. The results indicated that the ZIF-8 had an identical appearance to that of pure-ZIF-8 or the pristine peptide-ZIF-8 (Fig. 2b–f). The crystallinity of peptide-ZIF-8 after the treatments was further determined by XRD. No obvious change in crystallinity was observed in the groups before and after each treatment (Fig. S3). The above results showed that ZIF-8 can preserve its morphology and crystallinity under these harsh conditions.
## 3.3. Determination of peptide structure and identity
Next, an acetic buffer solution (pH 5) was used to dissolve ZIF-8 and release the peptide drugs. The release kinetics of peptides from ZIF-8 at $\mathsf{p H}7.4$ and $\mathsf{p H}~5$ were measured by checking the fluorescence-labeled peptides in the supernatants. The results were fitted by a Korsmeyer–Peppas model, which showed a quick release of peptides into the $\mathsf{p H}5$ solution (approximately $30\mathrm{mins})$ but a negligible release into the $\mathsf{p H}7.4$ condition (Fig. 3a). The kinetics constant (k) was significantly higher in the $\mathsf{p H}5$ solution than in the $\mathsf{p H}7.4$ group, while the release component (n) in both groups was lower than 0.43, indicating a Fickian diffusion in this case (Table S1). The obtained solution was then dialyzed against phosphate buffer $\mathrm{(pH7.4,50mM)}$ for $^{8\mathrm{~h~}}$ in an ice-bath (molecular weight cut-off of dialysis bag: 1 kDa) to remove $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ and 2-HmIm residuals. The concentration of the peptide drugs in solution was then determined by measuring the absorbance at $205\ \mathrm{nm}$ (the characteristic absorbance of the peptide bond). The total recovery efficiency of the peptide drugs during the encapsulation and release process was approximately $83\%$ (Table S2). Circular dichroism (CD) spectroscopy was then used to analyze the secondary structure of the peptide. The resulting spectra were normalized by the concentration of each peptide and the number of residuals. Hence, the signal intensity is only dependent on the structure. The groups with MOF protection had a higher signal in the secondary structure (coil: negative peaks near $195\mathrm{nm}$ and very low ellipticity above $210\mathrm{nm}$ ) [37] than those without MOF but had a lower signal than that of the untreated peptides, which further confirmed that the MOF encapsulation could mitigate the damage to the peptides from harsh conditions (Fig. 3b). To further determine if the peptide drugs maintained their complete sequence, liquid chromatography mass spectrometry (LC-MS) was employed to analyze the composition of the released peptide drugs. The results showed that the released peptide from ZIF-8 after exposure to different treatments shared identical peaks with that of the untreated peptide solution. The multiple charged peptide ions $739.69040+6\mathrm{\}$ $887.4821\div5$ , etc., were identified, and these peaks indicated the molecular weight of the solute was approximately $4.4\mathrm{\kDa}$ , which is the same as that of peptide 46 (Fig. 3c-f).
## 3.4. Determination of the biological activities from the released peptides
After characterization of the released peptides, cell viability experiments were performed to verify if the released peptides retained their biological activity by inducing the programmed death of cancer cells. Peptide 46 is a p53 activator that can upregulate the expression of the tumor suppressor gene p53 in some cancer cells (wild-type p53 and some mutant p53) to trigger the apoptosis signaling pathway [38]. MCF-7 breast cancer cells were used in this study due to their expression of the wild type p53 gene. Eight cell apoptosis experiments were carried out in parallel, namely: P-MOF-HT, P-MOF-FT, P-MOF-E, P, P-HT, P-FT, P-E, and a control group (no peptide added). The same number of cells was seeded into 24-well plates for $24\mathrm{h}.$ . We then treated the cells with the different peptides at the same concentration $(20~\upmu\up M)$ for another $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ . After that, the WST-1 kit was used to determine the cell viability [39]. The results clearly indicated that the peptides released from MOFs after various harsh conditions could still significantly inhibit the growth of MCF-7 cells, while free peptides without the protection of MOFs lost their biological activity of killing cancer cells (all the results were normalized to the control group without any peptides, Fig. 4a). The results were also supported by the live/dead cell staining experiments. Compared with the control group (no peptides added, Fig. S4), nearly half of the cells were stained red in the untreated peptide group (live and dead cells were stained green and red, respectively, Fig. 4b). Similar results were also observed in the groups with peptides released from MOF particles after various extreme treatments (Fig. 4c–e). To rule out that any residual of ZIF-8 had an adverse impact on cell proliferation, we first analyzed the SEM images of the sample solution before and after the dialysis process. Before the dialysis, most of the MOFs had been dissolved, and only very little debris could be found in the SEM images (Fig. S5a), while after the dialysis, no debris could be found in the SEM images (Fig. S5b). After that, we assessed the cytotoxicity of pure ZIF-8 on MCF-7 cells. After culturing the cells with ZIF-8 $(20\upmu\mathrm{g}/\mathrm{mL})$ for $48\mathrm{h}$ , the live/dead cell staining results showed negligible cytotoxicity on MCF-7 cells (Fig. S6). This observation is also supported by other groups’ research reporting the negligible cytotoxicity of ZIF-8 on other cell types [40–42]. To further verify whether it was the peptides inducing cell apoptosis through activating the p53 proteins, we first determined the cellular uptake of the peptides. As evaluated by fluorescence microscopy, the Atto-647 NHS ester-labeled-peptides accumulated around the cell nucleus and showed a high cellular uptake efficiency (Fig. S7). After that, the p53 expression level inside of the cells was determined by qPCR. A higher expression level was found in the P-MOF-E, P-MOF-HT, P-MOF-FT and P groups (the results were normalized to the control group where no peptide was added, Fig. 4f). All the above results indicated that the MOFs could protect peptide 46 from extreme conditions and preserve its biological activity of inhibiting the growth of cancer cells.
## 4. Conclusion
In conclusion, an efficient and facile method offered by the excellent protective properties of MOF matrices was applied to protect both chemically and physically unstable peptide drugs. This MOF-based nanoencapsulation had a higher loading efficiency under mild synthesis conditions and a better protection ability compared to traditional nanoencapsulation [16,43]. Peptide 46 encapsulated into ZIF-8 preserved its biological activities under high temperature, freeze-thaw cycles and enzyme degradation. After release from ZIF-8, the peptides retained their native functionality by enhancing p53 gene expression and inhibiting the growth of MCF-7 cells. The outstanding protective performance makes biomineralized MOF a versatile platform for protecting peptide drugs, and this study provides an alternative method for the storage and transportation of therapeutic peptide drugs. Looking to the future, another challenge remaining for peptide therapeutics is their general instability in strong acidic environments and hence their unsuitability for direct oral delivery. Further studies on MOF chemistry and the peptide-MOF interface could help the discovery of new MOF-based nanomatrices suitable for in vivo peptide delivery applications.
